SDP -- construct a semidefinite program
***************************************

  * Usage: 
        P = sdp(C,A,b)
        P = sdp(var,M,objFun)
  * Inputs:
      * C, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a symmetric $n\times n$
        matrix
      * A, a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,, consisting of $m$
        symmetric $n\times n$ matrices
      * b, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a $m\times 1$ matrix
      * var, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of variables
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, constraint matrix with
        affine-linear entries
      * objFun, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, linear
        function to be minimized
  * Outputs:
      * P, an instance of the type *note SDP: SDP,, a semidefinite programming
        problem

Description
===========

A semidefinite program (SDP) is defined by a some symmetric matrices $C, A_i$
and a vector $b$. The SDP has a primal and a dual problem. The primal problem
is

$$min_{X} \, C \bullet X \,\,\, s.t. \,\,\, A_i \bullet X = b_i \, and \, X
\geq 0$$

and the dual problem is

$$max_{y,Z} \, \sum_i b_i y_i \,\,\, s.t. \,\,\, Z = C - \sum_i y_i A_i \, and
\, Z \geq 0$$

The type *note SDP: SDP, stores semidefinite programs. There are two ways to
construct an SDP. The first option is to provide the matrices $C,A_i,b$.

+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : P = sdp(matrix{{1,0},{0,2}}, matrix{{0,1},{1,0}}, matrix{{-1}})|
|                                                                    |
|o1 = SDP{"A" => 1 : (| 0 1 |)}                                      |
|                     | 1 0 |                                        |
|         "b" => | -1 |                                              |
|         "C" => | 1 0 |                                             |
|                | 0 2 |                                             |
|                                                                    |
|o1 : SDP                                                            |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+

The second option is to provide a matrix $M(v)$, with affine entries, and a
linear function $f(v)$. This constructs an SDP in dual form: minimize $f(v)$
subject to $M(v)\geq 0$.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : R = QQ[u,v,w];                            |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = matrix {{1,u,3-v},{u,5,w},{3-v,w,9+u}}|
|                                               |
|o3 = | 1    u -v+3 |                           |
|     | u    5 w    |                           |
|     | -v+3 w u+9  |                           |
|                                               |
|             3      3                          |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R                           |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : objFun = u+v+w;                           |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : P = sdp({u,v,w}, M, objFun);              |
+-----------------------------------------------+

Semidefinite programs can be solved numerically using the method *note
optimize: optimize,, and in small cases also symbolically with the method *note
criticalIdeal: criticalIdeal,.



Methods that use an object of class SDP:
========================================

  * "criticalIdeal(SDP)" -- see *note criticalIdeal: criticalIdeal, -- ideal of
    critical equations of a semidefinite program
  * "criticalIdeal(SDP,ZZ)" -- see *note criticalIdeal: criticalIdeal, -- ideal
    of critical equations of a semidefinite program
  * "optimize(SDP)" -- see *note optimize: optimize, -- solve a semidefinite
    program
  * "optimize(SDP,Matrix)" -- see *note optimize: optimize, -- solve a
    semidefinite program
  * *note refine(SDP,Sequence): refine_lpSDP_cmSequence_rp, -- refine an SDP
    solution
  * "ring(SDP)"

